<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmdoc|english>>

<\body>
  <tmdoc-title|Choosing the style of your document>

  Every <TeXmacs> document has a <em|style>, which determines its global
  layout: the fonts, the margins, the way the sections are numbered and
  displayed, and so on. A new document has the <tmstyle|generic> style. The
  other standard styles are available in the menu <menu|Document|Style>:

  <\description>
    <item*|<tmstyle|article>>for articles and reports;

    <item*|<tmstyle|book>>for books, which are divided into chapters;

    <item*|<tmstyle|letter>>for letters, with headers for the addresses of
    the sender and the recipient;

    <item*|<tmstyle|beamer>>for presentations made of slides;

    <item*|<tmstyle|seminar>>for transparencies, with large fonts;

    <item*|<tmstyle|exam>>for exams (in the <menu|Education> submenu);

    <item*|<tmstyle|source>>for writing your own style files.
  </description>

  There are also styles for the articles of many scientific journals and
  publishers.

  For instance, in order to write an article, select
  <menu|Document|Style|article>. The document immediately takes the layout
  of an article: its title, abstract and sections (from the <menu|Insert>
  menu) are now typeset as in an article.

  The style mostly matters for the <em|layout> of your document: you write
  the same text in the same way whatever the style, and you may change the
  style at any moment. This is one of the main principles of <TeXmacs>, to
  which we will come back in the chapter on structured text.

